Reason:
The `readbuffers` parameter is being set to a default value of `2` without checking if the `V4L2_CAP_READWRITE` capability is enabled. This can cause compliance issues with `v4l2-compliance` for devices that do not support read/write operations, leading to warnings or errors.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
if (vfd->device_caps & V4L2_CAP_READWRITE)
    p->parm.capture.readbuffers = 2;
```
Reason:
This code checks if the `V4L2_CAP_READWRITE` capability is set before assigning a value to `readbuffers`. By doing so, it ensures that `readbuffers` is only set to `2` for devices that support read/write operations, thus preventing compliance issues with `v4l2-compliance`.


